$contact='<p class="intro">Tell us about your upcoming exhibition program, your ideal customers and what success would look like. We will explore whether a focused commercial intelligence approach fits your goals.</p><div class="content-grid"><div class="content-card"><h3>Discuss your next event</h3><p>Share the event, the audiences you care about, and what your current team does to identify targets.</p></div><div class="content-card"><h3>Explore a targeted pilot</h3><p>Consider a scoped engagement around one event and a carefully selected group of priority accounts.</p></div></div><h2 id="schedule">Start a conversation</h2><p>Schedule a strategy call through our current Agency 8200 calendar. We will discuss your event program, priorities and whether a scoped pilot makes sense.</p>[binteva_contact]';
$pages = array(
'how-it-works'=>array('title'=>'How Binteva works','excerpt'=>'A practical workflow from exhibitor data to prioritized, researched accounts and stronger commercial conversations.','content'=> '<p class="intro">Binteva is designed to transform an overwhelming event universe into a manageable, evidence-backed commercial plan. The goal is not more names. It is more clarity about which conversations could matter.</p><div class="section-divider"></div><span class="number-line">STEP 01</span><h2>Start with your business, not the event list</h2><p>Define your product lines, ideal customer profile, geography, deal dynamics, exclusions and target partner types. A manufacturer selling industrial equipment needs a different opportunity model than a logistics provider or a packaging specialist.</p><div class="content-grid"><div class="content-card"><h3>Customer configuration</h3><p>Products, market segments, technical requirements, territories and strategic priorities.</p></div><div class="content-card"><h3>Clear qualification criteria</h3><p>Transparent screening criteria shaped by your own sales organization.</p></div></div><span class="number-line">STEP 02</span><h2>Build a reliable exhibitor universe</h2><p>Import customer-authorized exhibitor directories, spreadsheets and permitted event data. Standardize company records, resolve duplicates, and flag gaps. We distinguish published exhibitor information from verified attendee data.</p><span class="number-line">STEP 03</span><h2>Screen broadly; investigate selectively</h2><p>Apply initial fit logic across the available universe, then move the most relevant candidates into deeper research. This layered approach keeps the work commercially useful instead of overwhelming the team with low-value dossiers.</p><span class="number-line">STEP 04</span><h2>Produce opportunity dossiers</h2><p>For priority accounts, develop company-level research covering relevant products, possible commercial needs, complementary capabilities, distribution and market fit, likely stakeholder roles, supporting sources, and open questions requiring validation.</p><span class="number-line">STEP 05</span><h2>Equip your sales team to act</h2><p>Translate research into account briefs, tailored conversation starters, meeting agendas, owner assignments and draft outreach. Your team reviews and approves all external communication.</p><span class="number-line">STEP 06</span><h2>Learn across exhibitions</h2><p>The roadmap includes retaining reusable account intelligence, recording outcomes, refreshing material facts and comparing the relevance of opportunities across events. CRM-connected attribution is a later-stage capability, not a current guaranteed feature.</p><div class="content-banner"><h2>Start with one meaningful exhibition.</h2><p>Explore a scoped opportunity intelligence pilot before considering a broader program.</p><a href="'.esc_url(home_url('/contact/')).'">Talk through a pilot ↗</a></div>'),
'why-binteva'=>array('title'=>'Why Binteva is different','excerpt'=>'Commercial relevance, evidence and action—not another badge scanner or generic lead list.','content'=>'<p class="intro">Most event technology helps you organize an event, discover attendees or capture leads. Binteva is designed to answer a more strategic question: which companies could create the most valuable commercial relationships for your business—and why?</p><div class="content-grid"><div class="content-card"><h3>Fit, not just firmographics</h3><p>Research into product alignment, distribution relevance, technical compatibility and commercial potential rather than employee count alone.</p></div><div class="content-card"><h3>Evidence, not black-box scores</h3><p>Explain why a company was prioritized using sources, research confidence and issues still to verify.</p></div><div class="content-card"><h3>Specificity, not generic outreach</h3><p>Build account-level talking points and engagement plans based on each company’s context.</p></div><div class="content-card"><h3>Continuity, not one-off lists</h3><p>Retain intelligence and institutional knowledge across a multi-event program as the platform develops.</p></div></div><h2>Designed to complement, not replace, your event stack</h2><p>Binteva is not positioned primarily as registration software, booth operations, attendee matchmaking or badge scanning. Those systems serve important purposes. Our focus is the research and commercial decision layer before, during and after an event.</p><h2>Built for complex industrial buying relationships</h2><p>For technical manufacturers and distributors, a prospect can be valuable because of an adjacent product line, a geographic channel gap, a compatible installed base or a partnership possibility. A generic lead score may miss that context.</p><h2>What we measure</h2><p>Relevant accounts accepted by sales, research time saved, meaningful conversations, opportunities created and credible pipeline contribution. Results must be evaluated against a customer-agreed baseline; we do not claim guaranteed meetings or revenue.</p>'),
'platform'=>array('title'=>'The Binteva platform','excerpt'=>'Research, qualification and opportunity execution designed around serious industrial B2B commercial teams.','content'=>'<p class="intro">One focused commercial intelligence layer for the decisions surrounding every major exhibition. The initial product centers on research and prioritization; activation workflows and CRM integrations are planned extensions.</p><div class="content-grid"><div class="content-card"><h3>01 / Company and event ingestion</h3><p>Import permitted exhibitor data, CSV and Excel files, standardize company names and reduce duplicate research.</p></div><div class="content-card"><h3>02 / Fit intelligence</h3><p>Evaluate account relevance against product categories, ideal customer profiles, exclusions and market fit criteria.</p></div><div class="content-card"><h3>03 / Deep opportunity dossiers</h3><p>Generate sourced research briefs, potential business-development angles and confidence indicators for priority companies.</p></div><div class="content-card"><h3>04 / Executive prioritization</h3><p>View screened companies, priority tiers, research progress and practical outputs in one structured workspace.</p></div><div class="content-card"><h3>05 / Sales activation roadmap</h3><p>Draft tailored outreach, assign account owners, build meeting plans and support human review and approval.</p></div><div class="content-card"><h3>06 / CRM and ROI roadmap</h3><p>Move approved information into the customer’s CRM and track pipeline outcomes through planned integrations.</p></div></div><h2>Illustrative engagement flow</h2><p>A sample event might begin with 1,500 available exhibitor records, identify 125 candidates for further review and produce 30–40 deeper priority dossiers. These figures illustrate a possible staged workflow, not Binteva customer performance.</p><h2>Trustworthy research is central</h2><p>Every substantive fit claim should be supported by evidence where feasible. Missing source data, ambiguous identities and unverified assumptions must be surfaced instead of treated as facts. Restricted directories and personal data should only be used where properly authorized.</p>'),
'industries'=>array('title'=>'Built for industrial B2B','excerpt'=>'Designed for organizations where complex products, large events and high-value relationships make research matter.','content'=>'<p class="intro">Binteva is initially focused on manufacturers, distributors and technical service providers with meaningful exhibition programs and high-value commercial relationships.</p><div class="content-grid"><div class="content-card"><h3>Industrial machinery & equipment</h3><p>Identify buyers, integrators, channel partners and complementary equipment manufacturers.</p></div><div class="content-card"><h3>Packaging & production technology</h3><p>Prioritize converters, OEMs, equipment suppliers and complementary solution providers.</p></div><div class="content-card"><h3>Industrial automation & electronics</h3><p>Research relevant applications, system integrators, technologies and distribution channels.</p></div><div class="content-card"><h3>Building materials & commercial HVAC</h3><p>Explore specification relationships, specialized distribution and market adjacency.</p></div><div class="content-card"><h3>Automotive aftermarket</h3><p>Discover channel fit, product portfolio overlap and potential distribution relationships.</p></div><div class="content-card"><h3>Logistics and specialized manufacturing</h3><p>Connect service capabilities and industry specialization to realistic commercial demand.</p></div></div><h2>Who tends to be a strong fit?</h2><p>Companies with substantial B2B deal values, recurring participation in major events, established sales teams and enough commercial complexity that better targeting changes outcomes. Binteva is likely less suitable for organizations primarily seeking simple attendee registration or mass-market badge capture.</p>'),
'about'=>array('title'=>'About Binteva','excerpt'=>'A sharper way to turn event participation into commercial opportunity intelligence.','content'=>'<p class="intro">Binteva was conceived around one belief: the value of a trade show should not be determined by how many booths a team visits or how many names enter a CRM. It should be determined by the quality of the commercial relationships the team can identify and develop.</p><h2>Why we exist</h2><p>Industrial companies invest heavily in exhibitions while pre-show research is often fragmented, inconsistent or late. Company lists are vast. Product fit is nuanced. Sales teams have limited time to research hundreds or thousands of potential accounts.</p><p>We are building a system to close that intelligence gap through account qualification, evidence-backed research, structured priorities and practical sales preparation.</p><h2>Our point of view</h2><div class="content-grid"><div class="content-card"><h3>Commercial context first</h3><p>Technology should understand what you sell, to whom, and why a relationship could be commercially meaningful.</p></div><div class="content-card"><h3>Transparency matters</h3><p>AI can accelerate research. Source evidence, confidence and human judgment make that research useful.</p></div><div class="content-card"><h3>Activation beats information overload</h3><p>Research is only valuable if the sales team knows what to do next.</p></div><div class="content-card"><h3>Results require validation</h3><p>We believe pilots should demonstrate relevance and workflow value before an enterprise makes a larger commitment.</p></div></div><p><strong>Product status:</strong> Binteva is presented as a proposed platform in validation and development. Capabilities described as planned or on the roadmap should not be interpreted as currently deployed functions.</p>'),
'faq'=>array('title'=>'Frequently asked questions','excerpt'=>'Practical answers about data, differentiation, research, rollout and the next step.','content'=>'<p class="intro">Here are the questions enterprise sales and marketing teams usually ask when evaluating an intelligence layer for their event program.</p><details><summary>Is Binteva an event registration or badge-scanning product?</summary><p>No. We concentrate on qualifying companies and explaining their possible commercial relevance. We expect to complement existing event-management and CRM tools.</p></details><details><summary>How is this different from buying a prospect list?</summary><p>A list gives you records. Binteva aims to explain product and market fit, prioritize accounts, surface supporting evidence and prepare a useful engagement strategy.</p></details><details><summary>Which industries are the best fit?</summary><p>Our initial focus is industrial B2B manufacturing and distribution, including machinery, packaging, automation, building materials, HVAC and adjacent technical markets.</p></details><details><summary>Can you guarantee qualified meetings or revenue?</summary><p>No. Research quality and targeted preparation can improve a process, but meetings, pipeline and closed revenue depend on factors beyond the software. Outcomes should be measured against agreed baselines.</p></details><details><summary>Where does the exhibitor information come from?</summary><p>Customer-provided data, authorized event directories and appropriately sourced public business information. We do not claim that a directory proves specific people will attend an event.</p></details><details><summary>Will the AI message people automatically?</summary><p>Our planned outreach features produce drafts and action recommendations. External messages require the customer’s review and approval.</p></details><details><summary>Can this integrate with our CRM?</summary><p>CRM-ready exports and later HubSpot and Salesforce integrations are part of the proposed roadmap. Confirm current availability in a product discussion.</p></details><details><summary>How quickly can we test the value?</summary><p>A proposed starting point is a paid, scoped pilot built around one upcoming event, subject to usable and properly authorized event data.</p></details><details><summary>Do you publish standard enterprise pricing?</summary><p>Packaging depends on event volume, research depth, company complexity and support needs. Discuss a pilot and a suitable commercial arrangement with us.</p></details>'),
'contact'=>array('title'=>'Let’s find the opportunity in your next event','excerpt'=>'See what a more focused, intelligence-led trade-show strategy could mean for your commercial team.','content'=>$contact)
);
$pages['commercial-value'] = array('title'=>'Make every exhibition investment count','excerpt'=>'A practical framework for measuring research efficiency, account quality and commercial outcomes.','content'=>'<p class="intro">Your event budget already funds the opportunity to meet the market. Better account intelligence aims to make the time your team spends there more useful.</p><h2>Measure the full chain of value</h2><div class="content-grid"><div class="content-card"><h3>Research efficiency</h3><p>Compare analyst hours, time to a usable shortlist and the percentage of records that need manual correction.</p></div><div class="content-card"><h3>Account relevance</h3><p>Track how many suggested companies sales accepts, why others are rejected and which evidence improves the decision.</p></div><div class="content-card"><h3>Commercial activation</h3><p>Record meetings prioritized, conversations completed, next steps agreed and qualified opportunities created.</p></div><div class="content-card"><h3>Financial contribution</h3><p>Separate pipeline from revenue. Attribute incremental gross profit only where the evidence supports it.</p></div></div><h2>An illustrative annual model</h2><p><strong>Assumptions only - not customer results, a quotation or a forecast.</strong> Suppose a team attends 12 shows at $50,000 each: a $600,000 annual event program. At an assumed intelligence fee of $15,000 per month, the annual fee would be $180,000.</p><div class="table-scroll"><table class="compare-table"><caption>Illustrative break-even sensitivity</caption><thead><tr><th>Gross profit per incremental win</th><th>Additional annual wins to cover $180,000</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>$30,000</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>$60,000</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>$90,000</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Break-even wins = annual fee ÷ incremental gross profit per win, rounded up. This excludes implementation, customer labor and other incremental costs; include those in a real evaluation. Existing event spend is context, not a claimed saving.</p><h2>Agree on a pilot scorecard</h2><p>Before work begins, define the event universe, baseline research process, acceptance criteria, account sample and measurement period. Review relevance first; assess pipeline and realized profit as sales cycles mature. Binteva does not guarantee meetings, pipeline or revenue.</p>');
$pages['platform']['content'] = '<p class="status-note"><strong>Product status:</strong> Binteva is in development. The website and sample interfaces describe the proposed product. No operational SaaS modules or integrations have been verified for general availability.</p>'.$pages['platform']['content'];
$pages['faq']['content'] .= '<details><summary>Is the software available today?</summary><p>Binteva is in development and validation. Contact us to discuss the proposed workflow and pilot suitability; confirm deliverables and timing before making a commitment.</p></details><details><summary>How is AI research verified?</summary><p>The proposed methodology retains source references, distinguishes facts from hypotheses and flags uncertain identity or incomplete data for human review. A score is a prioritization aid, not proof of buying intent.</p></details><details><summary>Do you need an attendee list?</summary><p>No. A permitted exhibitor directory can provide the company universe. It does not prove that a specific person will attend. Authorized attendee data may supplement the research when available.</p></details><details><summary>How will security and implementation work?</summary><p>Data access, retention, contractual terms and technical controls must be agreed for each pilot. No certification or enterprise security audit is claimed. Implementation begins with your commercial criteria, authorized source data and a defined review process.</p></details>';
$pages['why-binteva']['content'] .= '<h2>Where the intelligence layer fits</h2><div class="table-scroll"><table class="compare-table"><thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Typical purpose</th><th>Binteva’s proposed focus</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Directory or purchased list</td><td>Identify organizations and contacts</td><td>Explain account relevance to your capabilities</td></tr><tr><td>Manual research</td><td>Investigate accounts individually</td><td>Structure repeatable research and review</td></tr><tr><td>Registration and badge scanning</td><td>Manage participation and capture interactions</td><td>Prioritize accounts before and after interactions</td></tr><tr><td>Matchmaking and sales intelligence</td><td>Discover connections and prospect information</td><td>Apply event context and specific industrial fit criteria</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Capabilities vary by vendor and configuration. This comparison describes common purposes, not an exhaustive claim about any competitor.</p>';
